Here is the full **testsmells.org 19-smell academic audit** for `UserRepositoryIntegrationTests.cs`.

> **Calibration note:** The class is marked `[TestCategory("Integration")]`, so Mystery Guest, Eager Test, and magic-number count assertions are evaluated with integration-test norms. Smells that remain problematic even in integration scope are still flagged.

---

## Summary Dashboard

| Severity | Smell Count | Affected Tests |
|----------|-------------|----------------|
| High | 3 | 3 |
| Medium | 1 | 1 |
| Low | 0 | 0 |
| **Total** | **4** | **4** |

---

## Findings by Severity

### ❶ Conditional Test Logic — **High**

**Smell:** `Conditional Test Logic` (testsmells.org core smell #1)
**Test:** `GetUser_ReturnsCorrectType` (line 94)

```csharp
if (loaded is PremiumUser premium)
{
    Assert.IsTrue(premium.DiscountRate > 0);   // path A
}
else
{
    Assert.AreEqual("Eve", loaded!.Name);       // path B
}
```

**Problem:** Only one branch executes per run. If production code changes and starts returning a `PremiumUser`, the `else` branch silently stops running—and vice-versa. This creates a hidden testing gap.

**Fix — split into two explicit tests:**

```csharp
[TestMethod]
public void GetUser_WhenStandard_ReturnsCorrectName()
{
    _repository.Insert(new User("eve@example.com", "Eve"));
    var loaded = _repository.GetByEmail("eve@example.com");

    Assert.IsNotNull(loaded);
    Assert.IsInstanceOfType<User>(loaded);
    Assert.AreEqual("Eve", loaded.Name);
}

[TestMethod]
public void GetUser_WhenPremium_HasPositiveDiscount()
{
    _repository.Insert(new PremiumUser("eve@example.com", "Eve", discountRate: 0.1m));
    var loaded = _repository.GetByEmail("eve@example.com");

    Assert.IsNotNull(loaded);
    Assert.IsInstanceOfType<PremiumUser>(loaded);
    Assert.IsTrue(((PremiumUser)loaded).DiscountRate > 0);
}
```

**Risk if unfixed:** One branch may never execute in CI, giving false confidence.

---

### ❷ Sleepy Test — **High**

**Smell:** `Sleepy Test` (testsmells.org core smell #3)
**Test:** `NotifyOnInsert_SendsEventAfterDelay` (line 82)

```csharp
Thread.Sleep(3000);   // ← blocks the thread for 3 seconds

Assert.IsTrue(_repository.WasNotificationSent(user.Id));
```

**Problem:** A hard 3-second sleep makes the test slow and non-deterministic. If the notification takes 3.1 s on a loaded CI agent, the test flakes. Even in an integration context this remains a smell—integration tests should use polling or event-based waits, not fixed delays.

**Fix — poll with a timeout:**

```csharp
[TestMethod]
public async Task NotifyOnInsert_SendsEventAfterDelay()
{
    var user = new User("dave@example.com", "Dave");
    _repository.Insert(user);

    var sent = await PollUntilAsync(
        () => _repository.WasNotificationSent(user.Id),
        timeout: TimeSpan.FromSeconds(5),
        interval: TimeSpan.FromMilliseconds(100));

    Assert.IsTrue(sent, "Notification was not sent within the timeout.");
}

private static async Task<bool> PollUntilAsync(
    Func<bool> condition, TimeSpan timeout, TimeSpan interval)
{
    var deadline = DateTime.UtcNow + timeout;
    while (DateTime.UtcNow < deadline)
    {
        if (condition()) return true;
        await Task.Delay(interval);
    }
    return false;
}
```

**Risk if unfixed:** Flaky CI failures and a 3 s tax on every suite run.

---

### ❸ Assertion-Free Test (Unknown Test) — **High**

**Smell:** `Unknown Test` / `Assertion-Free Test` (testsmells.org core smell #4)
**Test:** `BulkInsert_RunsWithoutErrors` (line 113)

```csharp
public void BulkInsert_RunsWithoutErrors()
{
    for (int i = 0; i < 100; i++)
    {
        _repository.Insert(new User($"bulk{i}@example.com", $"Bulk User {i}"));
    }
    // ← no assertion at all
}
```

**Problem:** This test passes even if `Insert` silently drops every row. The name implies "no errors" but the framework would pass it even with a broken `Insert` as long as no exception is thrown.

**Fix — assert the observable outcome:**

```csharp
[TestMethod]
public void BulkInsert_PersistsAllRecords()
{
    for (int i = 0; i < 100; i++)
    {
        _repository.Insert(new User($"bulk{i}@example.com", $"Bulk User {i}"));
    }

    var users = _repository.ListAll();
    Assert.AreEqual(100, users.Count, "All 100 bulk-inserted users should be persisted.");
}
```

**Risk if unfixed:** False confidence — coverage looks good but nothing is actually verified.

---

### ❹ Assertion Roulette — **Medium**

**Smell:** `Assertion Roulette` (testsmells.org extended smell)
**Test:** `InsertUser_PersistsToDatabase` (line 28)

```csharp
Assert.IsNotNull(loaded);           // which one failed?
Assert.AreEqual("Alice", loaded.Name);
Assert.AreEqual("alice@example.com", loaded.Email);
Assert.IsTrue(loaded.Id > 0);       // four assertions, zero messages
```

**Problem:** Four assertions with no failure messages. When one fails in CI, the log shows only `Assert.AreEqual failed. Expected:<Alice>. Actual:<null>` with no context about *which property* was being checked or why.

**Fix — add descriptive messages:**

```csharp
Assert.IsNotNull(loaded, "User should be retrievable after insert.");
Assert.AreEqual("Alice", loaded.Name, "Name should survive round-trip.");
Assert.AreEqual("alice@example.com", loaded.Email, "Email should survive round-trip.");
Assert.IsTrue(loaded.Id > 0, "Auto-generated Id should be positive.");
```

**Risk if unfixed:** Slower triage when a CI failure requires reading source to find which assertion broke.

---

## Smell-Free Patterns ✅

The remaining **4 tests** are well-constructed:

| Test | Why it's clean |
|------|---------------|
| `UpdateUser_ModifiesExistingRecord` | Clear arrange-act-assert, 2 focused assertions |
| `DeleteUser_RemovesFromDatabase` | Single assertion on the expected absence — clean |
| `ListUsers_ReturnsAllInserted` | Count assertion (`3`) is self-documenting (3 inserts immediately above) |
| `InsertUser_ConcurrentInserts_MaintainsIntegrity` | Proper async/await, clear assertion, `10` is contextually obvious |

The `[TestInitialize]`/`[TestCleanup]` fixture is also clean: both `_connection` and `_repository` are used by every test (no **General Fixture** smell), and the in-memory SQLite pattern provides hermetic isolation (no **Mystery Guest** smell despite using a real database).

---

## Prioritized Remediation Plan

| Priority | Smell | Fix | Effort |
|----------|-------|-----|--------|
| 1 | **Assertion-Free Test** (`BulkInsert_RunsWithoutErrors`) | Add `Assert.AreEqual(100, users.Count)` | ~2 min |
| 2 | **Conditional Test Logic** (`GetUser_ReturnsCorrectType`) | Split into two deterministic tests | ~10 min |
| 3 | **Sleepy Test** (`NotifyOnInsert_SendsEventAfterDelay`) | Replace `Thread.Sleep` with poll-until helper | ~15 min |
| 4 | **Assertion Roulette** (`InsertUser_PersistsToDatabase`) | Add message strings to all 4 assertions | ~3 min |

**Priority rationale:** Fix #1 first — it's the only test giving completely false confidence (passes regardless of correctness). Fixes #2 and #3 eliminate flakiness and hidden gaps. Fix #4 is cosmetic but improves CI triage speed.
